WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:08.720
Fran: When you've got a young person whose
family is say, bringing them to sessions

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.350
and who referred them to sessions and who
talks to them about their mental health.

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:16.580
It's amazing to see how much difference
that makes, because if I'm working with

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:21.675
someone - I'm working with a young man at
the minute, for instance, who I'm talking

00:00:21.675 --> 00:00:25.035
to his mum a couple of times a month as
well, and getting her to go through the

00:00:25.055 --> 00:00:27.854
stuff that we go through in sessions with
him, because that's really helping him

00:00:27.854 --> 00:00:31.005
to - his problem is that you can't, he
struggles to talk to people, he struggles

00:00:31.005 --> 00:00:32.625
to share, he doesn't want to worry people.

00:00:32.985 --> 00:00:35.685
So bringing his mum into that and
her being very involved in the work

00:00:35.685 --> 00:00:39.735
that we do is helping him to realise,
okay, well, I don't, I can't only

00:00:39.735 --> 00:00:43.175
do this when I have my session with
Fran, I can also do it with my mum.

00:00:43.455 --> 00:00:47.805
Tamzin: When young people have got good
relationships with parents, carers,

00:00:47.805 --> 00:00:53.805
and siblings, you often find that they
actually probably take what we give

00:00:53.805 --> 00:00:57.885
them in terms of the therapy tools a
lot better, because they've got those

00:00:57.885 --> 00:01:00.025
kind of people to lean on as well.

00:01:00.175 --> 00:01:03.425
Fran: As a mental health worker, anywhere
ever in the country at the minute, really

00:01:03.425 --> 00:01:07.500
we're limited to how many sessions we can
give to people, so, I think there's a big

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.380
difference between knowing that you're
leaveing someone when you're finished

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:13.860
working with them, you're leaving them
with a secure family attachment that can

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:16.140
help them to continue their progress.

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:19.440
Or if you're finishing with someone who
doesn't really have that support network

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:23.445
of a family, that they can sometimes,
you know, you worry that they're not

00:01:23.445 --> 00:01:24.735
gonna be able to continue that progress.

00:01:24.735 --> 00:01:26.115
'Cause they're going to be
back to the place where they

00:01:26.115 --> 00:01:27.104
haven't really got anyone.

00:01:27.664 --> 00:01:30.455
Tamzin: When they haven't got that
relationship with their parents.

00:01:30.545 --> 00:01:33.604
And pretty much, you know, it's
50/50, with a lot of young people

00:01:33.604 --> 00:01:37.265
we work with, these young people
can't talk to their parents.

00:01:37.445 --> 00:01:39.515
So you often find them more isolated.

00:01:39.785 --> 00:01:42.215
You often find that their
problems are a lot worse.

00:01:42.244 --> 00:01:44.975
I've took young people to A&E
because they've not been able

00:01:44.975 --> 00:01:46.294
to talk to their mum or dad.

00:01:46.414 --> 00:01:49.154
Fran: Sometimes parents you work with
who really want to be supportive and

00:01:49.154 --> 00:01:50.405
are struggling with things themselves.

00:01:50.405 --> 00:01:54.270
But I think even that, you know, they're
having a hard time, but they want to help

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:56.070
and they want to be there for their kid.

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:59.610
It can just, it can just make, it can
make all the difference really between

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:04.530
a sort of successful recovery and, you
know, somebody who needs to keep coming

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:06.090
back for different types of support.

